The 12k's G5 gave back all of that gift: the lead fell from +46.1 to +16.4, another loss of 29.8 points. The engine again chose B5, the same point it had been pointing at since move 51."}, {"turn": 65, "text": "Black's F1 swung the game by 29.3 points, from +20.3 to -9.0, which put White ahead. It was one of a run of near-30-point swings. White's H5 on turn 66 undid it, losing 29.6 points as the lead went from -9.0 back to +20.6."}, {"turn": 72, "text": "White's A5 was the move that settled it. It went from -7.7 to -7.9, a gain of 0.2 points, at the end of the exchanges that began at move 60. From here the lead stayed between -7.7 and -9.7 until the passes, and the left edge from A2 to A5 ended in White's hands."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and flood-fills each group to print its stones and liberty count, flagging groups with 2 or fewer liberties as DANGER. It never tries candidate moves or looks ahead, so it only inspects the current position.", "stop": false}}